-- Q18 [Sympathetic vs Parasympathetic | medium | Skill 2 | ans D]
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES (
    'bio_biochem', 'The Nervous System', $sub18$Sympathetic vs Parasympathetic$sub18$, $qt18$During cardiac surgery, a patient's vagus (parasympathetic) nerve input to the SA node is pharmacologically blocked while sympathetic activity stays at its baseline resting level. A physiologist predicts how the patient's heart rate will change from its previous resting value of about 70 bpm.$qt18$, $op18$[{"label": "A", "text": "It falls below 60 bpm, because vagal input supplies the drive that keeps the SA node firing."}, {"label": "B", "text": "It stays near 70 bpm, because the sympathetic division alone sets the resting heart rate."}, {"label": "C", "text": "It climbs to 120–140 bpm, the level the sympathetic division produces during exercise."}, {"label": "D", "text": "It rises toward about 100 bpm, because the SA node returns to its intrinsic firing rate."}]$op18$::jsonb, 'D', $ex18$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Nervous System Organization'. The answer is D because removing parasympathetic acetylcholine lets the SA node depolarize to threshold more readily, so the rate rises from the resting 60–80 bpm range toward the node's intrinsic rate of about 100 bpm. The text states that ACh binds a muscarinic receptor "that causes the cells to hyperpolarize so that they cannot reach threshold as easily, and the heart rate slows. Without this parasympathetic input, the heart would work at a rate of approximately 100 beats per minute (bpm)." It further explains the resting rate "is the result of the parasympathetic system slowing the heart down from its intrinsic rate of 100 bpm," and that "decreasing parasympathetic stimulation... allows HR to increase up to approximately 100 bpm. Any increases beyond this rate would require sympathetic stimulation." Because sympathetic activity here is unchanged at baseline, the rate settles at the intrinsic ~100 bpm. (Choice A) is wrong because vagal/parasympathetic input slows the heart rather than driving it, so removing it speeds the heart up, not down. (Choice B) is wrong because the resting rate reflects parasympathetic tone slowing the intrinsic rate, not sympathetic control setting it. (Choice C) is wrong because 120–140 bpm requires active sympathetic stimulation, which is unchanged here; loss of parasympathetic input only restores the ~100 bpm intrinsic rate. (Choice D) is correct because without ongoing parasympathetic ACh the pacemaker runs near its intrinsic rate of about 100 bpm. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because it requires predicting the directional and quantitative outcome on heart rate from the removal of one autonomic input while holding the other constant.$ex18$, 'medium', '3A', $cc18$Nervous System Organization$cc18$, 'biology', 2, 105
  ) RETURNING id
)
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype)
SELECT id, 'A', 'reversed_relationship', $a18A$Treating an inhibitory input as the excitatory drive, so removing it is predicted to lower rather than raise heart rate$a18A$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'B', 'misconception', $a18B$Attributing the resting set point to sympathetic control rather than to parasympathetic tone over the intrinsic rate$a18B$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'C', 'partial_truth', $a18C$Citing a real sympathetic exercise range as the outcome of merely losing parasympathetic input$a18C$ FROM q;

-- Q19 [Sympathetic vs Parasympathetic | medium | Skill 2 | ans C]
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES (
    'bio_biochem', 'The Nervous System', $sub19$Sympathetic vs Parasympathetic$sub19$, $qt19$In dim light, the sympathetic system activates a postganglionic fiber from the superior cervical ganglion that projects to the radial smooth muscle of the iris, contracting it to dilate the pupil. A pharmacologist wants to know what this postganglionic fiber releases onto the radial muscle to produce that effect.$qt19$, $op19$[{"label": "A", "text": "It releases acetylcholine, which binds muscarinic receptors on the radial smooth muscle."}, {"label": "B", "text": "It releases epinephrine directly from the nerve ending onto the radial smooth muscle."}, {"label": "C", "text": "It releases norepinephrine, which binds adrenergic receptors on the radial smooth muscle."}, {"label": "D", "text": "It releases nitric oxide directly from the nerve ending onto the radial smooth muscle."}]$op19$::jsonb, 'C', $ex19$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Nervous System Organization'. The answer is C because postganglionic sympathetic fibers, including the one from the superior cervical ganglion that projects to the iris, release norepinephrine, which binds adrenergic receptors on the radial smooth muscle to cause contraction and pupil dilation. The text states that the postganglionic fiber 'releases norepinephrine onto the radial fibers of the iris (a smooth muscle). When those fibers contract, the pupil dilates,' and that 'Norepinephrine results in dilation and ACh results in constriction.' The general rule is that postganglionic sympathetic fibers release norepinephrine that binds adrenergic receptors, whereas the parasympathetic fiber to the iris releases ACh onto the circular fibers to constrict the pupil; this contrast in transmitter (norepinephrine vs. ACh), not a shared one, is what distinguishes the two divisions at this effector. (Choice A) Acetylcholine bound to muscarinic receptors is the parasympathetic postganglionic transmitter that constricts the pupil, and ACh is also the preganglionic sympathetic transmitter, so it is not what this postganglionic sympathetic fiber releases. (Choice B) Epinephrine is secreted by the adrenal medulla into the bloodstream as a hormone, not released at the postganglionic synapse, where norepinephrine is the transmitter. (Choice C) Correct: the postganglionic sympathetic fiber releases norepinephrine, which acts on adrenergic receptors of the radial muscle to dilate the pupil. (Choice D) Nitric oxide is released directly as a neurotransmitter by only a few neurons (and cholinergic neurons trigger endothelial NO for vasodilation), not by the sympathetic fiber innervating the iris radial muscle. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because it requires inferring the specific postganglionic sympathetic transmitter at a named effector and distinguishing it from the parasympathetic transmitter and from hormonal/other signaling molecules.$ex19$, 'medium', '3A', $cc19$Nervous System Organization$cc19$, 'biology', 2, 105
  ) RETURNING id
)
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype)
