UNION ALL SELECT id, 'C', 'adjacent_fact', $a21C$Substituting the three-neuron ascending dorsal column sensory pathway to the brain for the local reflex arc$a21C$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'D', 'reversed_relationship', $a21D$Misnaming the two cells the single synapse connects (two motor neurons instead of a sensory and a motor neuron)$a21D$ FROM q;

-- Q22 [Reflex Arcs | medium | Skill 2 | ans B]
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES (
    'bio_biochem', 'The Nervous System', $sub22$Reflex Arcs$sub22$, $qt22$A neurophysiologist diagrams the hot-stove withdrawal reflex and notes that the sensory neuron carrying the pain signal does not contact the motor neuron directly; instead, its axon synapses onto an interneuron in the spinal cord gray matter, and that interneuron in turn synapses onto the motor neuron driving the response. Based on this wiring, the investigator should classify the arc as:$qt22$, $op22$[{"label": "A", "text": "a monosynaptic reflex arc, because the sensory neuron synapses directly onto the motor neuron"}, {"label": "B", "text": "a polysynaptic reflex arc, because at least one interneuron lies between the sensory and motor neurons"}, {"label": "C", "text": "an autonomic reflex arc, because the painful stimulus triggers an involuntary protective response"}, {"label": "D", "text": "a cranial reflex arc, because the integration of the signal occurs in the brain stem"}]$op22$::jsonb, 'B', $ex22$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Nervous System Organization'. The answer is B because the defining feature of a reflex arc's classification is the number of synapses between the sensory (afferent) and motor (efferent) neurons: when one or more interneurons are interposed, the arc is polysynaptic. In the hot-stove withdrawal reflex, the text states that the inhibition of the antagonist 'occurs through an interneuron in the spinal cord... The interneuron receives a synapse from the axon of the sensory neuron... the interneuron then inhibits the motor neuron' (A&P 2e, lines 26846-26849), so the sensory-to-motor pathway is bridged by an interneuron and is therefore polysynaptic. A monosynaptic arc, by contrast, is one 'in which the sensory neuron directly activates the motor neuron' (A&P 2e, line 28059), which describes the stretch (knee-jerk) reflex, not this withdrawal circuit. (Choice A) is wrong because the described wiring explicitly inserts an interneuron between sensory and motor neurons, so the sensory neuron does not synapse directly onto the motor neuron as a monosynaptic arc requires. (Choice B) is correct because the interposed spinal interneuron makes this a multi-synapse (polysynaptic) pathway from sensory input to motor output. (Choice C) is wrong because withdrawal is a somatic reflex 'involving skeletal muscle contractions' (A&P 2e, lines 21230-21233), not an autonomic (visceral) reflex acting on smooth muscle, cardiac muscle, or glands. (Choice D) is wrong because spinal somatic reflexes are integrated in the spinal cord—'The coordination of reflexes depends on the integration of sensory and motor pathways in the spinal cord' (A&P 2e, line 23405)—not in the brain stem as a cranial reflex would be. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because the student must apply the mono- versus polysynaptic classification rule to a specific described circuit and infer the correct category from the wiring rather than recall a stated definition.$ex22$, 'medium', '3A', $cc22$Nervous System Organization$cc22$, 'biology', 2, 105
  ) RETURNING id
)
