UNION ALL SELECT id, 'B', 'reversed_relationship', $a24B$Swaps which arc depends on the interneuron, claiming the monosynaptic knee-jerk requires it and the polysynaptic withdrawal does not$a24B$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'C', 'partial_truth', $a24C$Correctly notes a direct sensory-to-motor synapse exists but wrongly extends it to both arcs, sparing the polysynaptic withdrawal reflex$a24C$ FROM q;

-- Q25 [Reflex Arcs | hard | Skill 2 | ans C]
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES (
    'bio_biochem', 'The Nervous System', $sub25$Reflex Arcs$sub25$, $qt25$A student touches a hot stove and her hand jerks away. Tracing the spinal circuit, an interneuron in the dorsal horn receives a synapse from the burned-hand sensory neuron and then acts on the motor neuron that drives the triceps brachii (the antagonist of the flexing biceps). What does this interneuron do to that triceps motor neuron, and why does it speed the withdrawal?$qt25$, $op25$[{"label": "A", "text": "It excites the biceps motor neuron so that the biceps contracts and flexes the arm away from the stove"}, {"label": "B", "text": "It hyperpolarizes the incoming sensory neuron so that the painful signal stops once the hand has withdrawn"}, {"label": "C", "text": "It hyperpolarizes the triceps motor neuron so that the triceps relaxes and the antagonist does not oppose withdrawal"}, {"label": "D", "text": "It excites the triceps motor neuron so that both muscles contract and stabilize the elbow during withdrawal"}]$op25$::jsonb, 'C', $ex25$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Nervous System Organization'. The answer is C because the spinal interneuron's specific job in the hot-stove withdrawal reflex is to inhibit (hyperpolarize) the motor neuron controlling the antagonist triceps brachii so that muscle relaxes. Because the neuromuscular junction is strictly excitatory and skeletal muscle cannot actively relax, the only way to silence the triceps is to inhibit its motor neuron; the text states the interneuron 'inhibits the motor neuron that controls the triceps brachii... by releasing a neurotransmitter or other signal that hyperpolarizes the motor neuron... With this motor neuron being inhibited, the triceps brachii relaxes' (A&P 2e, lines 26849-26852). Removing the antagonistic triceps contraction lets the biceps withdraw the hand faster, limiting tissue damage. (Choice A) is wrong because the biceps is driven directly by the sensory neuron exciting its own ventral-horn motor neuron, not by this inhibitory interneuron (lines 25285-25287). (Choice B) is wrong because the interneuron synapses onto a motor neuron, not back onto the incoming sensory neuron. (Choice C) is correct because hyperpolarizing the triceps motor neuron relaxes the antagonist and removes the resistance that would otherwise slow withdrawal. (Choice D) is wrong because exciting the triceps would cause co-contraction that stabilizes rather than withdraws the arm, the reverse of the interneuron's actual inhibitory effect. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because it requires chaining the excitatory-only nature of the neuromuscular junction to the necessity of inhibiting the antagonist's motor neuron and predicting the functional consequence for withdrawal speed.$ex25$, 'hard', '3A', $cc25$Nervous System Organization$cc25$, 'biology', 2, 160
  ) RETURNING id
)
